is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the 10.5.0.0/24 segment and its default gateway (10.5.0.254), which resides on sshswitch (interface br_v5). End-to-end tests from both affected hosts (PC1, PC2) showed 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond RTT to 10.5.0.254 over multiple runs (30–50 pings each). ARP on PC1 resolves 10.5.0.254 consistently to 5e:18:eb:4d:e6:f2, and arping responses are steady (<1 ms), indicating no ARP instability.

On sshswitch, br_v5 (10.5.0.254/24) is UP; the trunk subinterface eth0.5 toward switch4 is forwarding with zero RX/TX errors or drops. Kernel logs show no link up/down events for eth0/eth0.5/br_v5, and the bond (eth6/eth7) has Link Failure Count 0 on both slaves. Switch4 (access switch for VLAN 5) shows br_vlan5 and ports eth1/eth2 (PC1/PC2) and the uplink eth0.5 all UP, forwarding, and free of errors/drops; no link flap events are logged. FDB tables on both sides show stable MAC learning for the gateway and hosts, with no evidence of MAC flapping.

Mechanism: If the gateway were flapping or overloaded, we would see link events, MAC churn, ARP instability, or packet loss—none are present. Localization: sshswitch br_v5 and the switch4 trunk/access ports are stable. Root cause: No network-side fault found; the reported “dropouts” were not reproducible and do not correlate with gateway interface flaps. Corrective fix: None required on the gateway; recommend client-side checks and, if desired, deploy short-interval ICMP monitoring to 10.5.0.254 to capture any future anomalies with timestamps. 
=== END ===